{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n\nDaily plan-review cache reads in `.rebar/usage.jsonl` went from ~80% of input tokens (2026-08-06..11) to 0 from 2026-08-12 onward, and the median full plan review went from about $2.18 to about $4.82 (Aug 6–29 logged spend is roughly $3,000). The change coincides with commit `6d4f394195b2` (2026-08-10, ticket `esthetical-earthborn-vervet` / `8fbd-e9d5-326e-40d8`) adding the Anthropic-direct fallback to the frontier/standard classes in `rebar.toml` `[llm.model_classes]`. `_intersect_capabilities` in `src/rebar/llm/runner_support.py` intersects capabilities across the whole chain and collapses differing `prompt_cache_style` values to `\"none\"`, so every call on a mixed chain runs uncached even though the primary Bedrock target supports caching.\n\nBoth mixed chains are **Bedrock-primary, Anthropic-fallback** (`rebar.toml:223-224`): `frontier = bedrock:...opus + fallback anthropic:...opus`; `standard` likewise for sonnet. The Anthropic hedge fires **0.43%** of calls (empirical: 51 of 11,902 logged rows), so the collapse disables caching on the Bedrock target that serves ~99.6% of calls.\n\n## Prior art this reverses, and why reversing is sound\n\nThe whole-chain collapse-to-none is documented/approved intent — `rebar.toml` ~190-193, authorizing ticket `8fbd`, and ADR 0059 §7 — resting on ONE premise: that a provider's cache keys \"would error on the candidate that does not share it,\" so a mixed chain must fail closed. **That premise is false for cache keys, proven at runtime against the pinned pydantic-ai:**\n\n- The cache directive is a set of provider-scoped `ModelSettings` keys (`bedrock_cache_*` vs `anthropic_cache_*`, `capabilities.py:664-716`). `ModelSettings` is a `TypedDict(total=False)` — no runtime key validation — and `pydantic_ai.settings.merge_model_settings` is a plain dict union (`base | overrides`), also no validation.\n- Each provider model reads ONLY its own keys via `model_settings.get('<prefix>_cache_*')`. `AnthropicModel` consults exactly four keys (`anthropic.py:1190,1580,1719,1766`); a `bedrock_cache_*` key is a silent `.get()` miss. So when the rare Anthropic fallback actually serves the Bedrock-primary directive, it applies NO caching, adds NO top-level `cache_control`, and cannot reach the `anthropic_cache`/`anthropic_cache_messages` mutual-exclusion `UserError`: **uncached-but-correct, never an error** (runtime-verified; mirrors LiteLLM, which \"silently skips\" an inapplicable cache directive with \"no error\").\n- The \"Bedrock rejects a top-level `cache_control`\" wire error (ADR 0059 §7; **LangChain#37042**) is the OPPOSITE case: it is produced only by `anthropic_cache=True` (Anthropic's automatic top-level key) reaching a Bedrock model — i.e. an Anthropic-PRIMARY chain. No chain in `rebar.toml` is Anthropic-primary, and even if one were, a Bedrock model never reads `anthropic_cache`, so the top-level `cache_control` is never emitted for it. LangChain#37042 argues FOR per-resolved-provider cache keys (which rebar already emits) and AGAINST a shared/automatic key — it does not support a chain-wide capability collapse.\n\nThe asymmetry, therefore: fields that CAN hard-fail cross-provider (`supports_temperature` → HTTP 400, `native_structured_output`, `supports_thinking`, `native_output_with_thinking`, `native_web_search`) STILL fail closed with `all(...)`; only `prompt_cache_style` — advisory and inert cross-provider — follows the PRIMARY. This aligns rebar with pydantic-ai's own model (`FallbackModel` re-prepares settings per resolved candidate) and LiteLLM's (caching is a property of the resolved serving model, `supports_prompt_caching(model=…)`), not a min-intersection across candidates. It preserves ADR 0059 §2's invariant: the decision reads the `prompt_cache_style` capability FIELD, never a provider-name string.\n\n## Approach\n\nOption **(a): fix the capability seam** (operator decision; option (b) — homogenising the chains — is OFF THE TABLE because the Anthropic hedge is required functionality). In `_intersect_capabilities`, derive `prompt_cache_style` from `per_candidate[0]` (the primary/serving candidate) instead of collapsing to `\"none\"` on disagreement; keep every hard-failing field conservatively `all(...)`-intersected. Update the now-stale documented intent in `rebar.toml` (~190-193) and ADR 0059 §7 to match. The runtime safety analysis above establishes (a) does NOT destabilise the seam, so no escalation.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/llm/runner_support.py` — the seam fix.\n- `tests/unit/test_runner_support.py` — NEW focused test module for `_intersect_capabilities` (the function lives in `runner_support.py`; existing incidental coverage in `tests/unit/test_llm_capabilities.py` imports it via the `rebar.llm.runner` re-export and asserts unrelated native-output-under-thinking behavior, which is unaffected).\n- `rebar.toml` — update the `[llm.model_classes]` caching comment.\n- `docs/adr/0059-llm-provider-seam-and-support-tiers.md` — superseding decision note in §7.\n\n## Testing\n\nUnit (`tests/unit/test_runner_support.py`):\n- Mixed Bedrock-primary + Anthropic-fallback chain keeps the primary's `\"bedrock\"` cache style (core regression; RED before the fix).\n- Symmetry: an Anthropic-primary chain keeps `\"anthropic\"`; a `\"none\"`-primary chain stays `\"none\"`.\n- Hard-fail fields (`supports_temperature`, `native_structured_output`) STILL intersect conservatively on a mixed chain (guards against over-broadening).\n- **Fallback-actually-fires safety**: `cache_settings_for` on the mixed chain emits only `bedrock_cache_*` keys, and merging them into the Anthropic arm's settings leaves every `anthropic_cache_*` read `None` and the mutual-exclusion `UserError` unreachable — codifying the ignored-not-errored proof for the branch where the Anthropic fallback serves.\n\nLive check: one `review-plan` after the fix shows `cache_read_tokens > 0` on second and later finder calls in `.rebar/usage.jsonl`.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] A regression test proves a mixed chain no longer collapses `prompt_cache_style` to none for the primary target; checked in `make test`.\n- [ ] A test proves the fallback-serves branch is safe (primary cache directive inert on the Anthropic arm, no error).\n- [ ] After the fix, a production plan review logs non-zero `cache_read_tokens`; checked in `.rebar/usage.jsonl`.\n- [ ] The stale documented intent (`rebar.toml` comment + ADR 0059 §7) is updated to match cache-follows-primary.\n- [ ] The root cause, prior-art engagement, and the chosen option are recorded on this ticket."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1788035499579698001,"uuid":"708cae34-78dd-48ae-b54b-a70c3182fcf5"}